WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.992
An AI agent attack surface includes every place where instructions, data, identities, or actions can enter or leave the system.

2
00:00:07.992 --> 00:00:23.318
Common components include system prompts, user messages, tools, APIs, plugins, retrieved documents, files, memory, credentials, external content, logs, model providers, and connected applications.

3
00:00:23.318 --> 00:00:27.788
Each component creates assumptions that should be tested rather than accepted silently.

4
00:00:27.788 --> 00:00:30.652
A useful threat model follows three paths.

5
00:00:30.652 --> 00:00:34.942
The instruction path asks who can influence what the agent believes it should do.

6
00:00:34.942 --> 00:00:41.468
The data path asks what information the agent can retrieve, retain, transform, or disclose.

7
00:00:41.468 --> 00:00:47.574
The action path asks which tools can change systems or communicate outside the application.

8
00:00:47.574 --> 00:00:55.147
A weakness becomes more serious when those paths connect, such as untrusted content influencing a tool that has broad access.

9
00:00:55.147 --> 00:00:57.781
Identity is part of the attack surface too.

10
00:00:57.781 --> 00:01:05.005
Teams should determine whether an action runs as the user, a shared service account, or a powerful application identity.

11
00:01:05.005 --> 00:01:11.960
Secrets, tokens, delegated credentials, and tool-specific permissions need clear ownership and rotation.

12
00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:20.072
Third-party models, connectors, libraries, and hosted services also introduce dependencies that should be inventoried and reviewed.

13
00:01:20.072 --> 00:01:25.500
The practical output of attack-surface analysis is a data-flow and trust-boundary map.

14
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:31.895
For every component, record what it receives, what it trusts, what it can access, and what it can change.

15
00:01:31.895 --> 00:01:43.649
That map helps teams place authorization, validation, isolation, rate limits, approvals, logging, and monitoring around the capabilities that could create meaningful harm.
